rderable, this implies \\(\\ell \\)-equitability for any strongly base orderable matroid.\n\nAn intriguing conjecture of the above form is Rota’s basis conjecture: given a matroid of rank r whose ground set partitions into r bases \\(B_1,B_2,\\ldots ,B_r\\), there always exists a transversal set of disjoint bases, i.e., \\(B'_1,B'_2,\\ldots ,B'_r\\) such that \\(|B_i\\cap B'_j|=1\\) for all \\(i,j\\in [r]\\). Note that this can be seen as a special instance of r-equitability.\n\nRegarding the general question, Aharoni and Berger [1] conjectured that if the ground set can be partitioned into k bases in both matroids, then it can be covered by at most \\(k+1\\) common independent sets. They showed the weaker bound that there always exists a partition into 2k common independent sets. This in particular shows that in Rota’s conjecture, the ground set can be covered by \\(\\le 2r\\) independent sets that intersect each \\(B_i\\) at most once. On the other hand, Bucić, Kwan, Pokrovskiy, and Sudakov [19] showed the existence of \\((\\frac{1}{2}-o(1))n\\) disjoint transversal bases, and Pokrovskiy [48] gave an asymptotic version showing the existence of \\((1-o(1))n\\) disjoint transversal independent sets of size \\((1-o(1))n\\). Recently, Montgomery and Sauermann [44] improved these results by proving the existence of \\((1-o(1))n\\) disjoint transversal bases.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCatalan pairs and Fishburn triples | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/271209491_Catalan_pairs_and_Fishburn_triples)\nciteturn8search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; This is accomplished via a bijective proof of the quadruple equidistribution of (\\asc,\\rep,\\zero,\\max) and (\\rep,\\asc,\\rmin,\\zero), where \\rmin denotes the right-to-left minima statistic of ascent sequences. (iii) an extension of a conjecture posed by Levande, which asserts that the pair (\\asc,\\zero) o